Write down what the report-only tests currently read

DRACO.md has said since August that a green suite does not mean nothing regressed, and named the
three tests that measure rather than assert. It did not say what they measure TO. So the rule was
unfollowable in practice: seeing `drake surface losses: 15 fields` tells a session nothing unless it
already knows whether 15 is the number.

That happened this session. The figure looked like it had moved — the alpha.6 notes record 16 — and
settling it took three greps back through the git-records to find that `324556c` had predicted the
`[reference package]` bucket disappearing, 16 to 15, with the other two buckets unchanged. The answer
was recorded; it was just recorded somewhere nobody would look. A table makes that a lookup.

Eight headlines, with the capture line beside them, since they go to the console logger rather than
to the per-suite files and are lost the moment the scrollback is:

    ExampleDomainsGenTest   28 match, 20 differ, 0 error, 0 missing (of 48)
    DrakeParseTest          15 surface-loss fields across 91 types — expression form 12, spelling 3
    DrakeParseTest          81 draco + 10 mods types in, 0 held back
    DrakeGenTest            0 mods actors pending .drake              (file-only)
    PonCorpusTest           80 numbers, 550 expressions, 42 discrepancies
    PonCorpusTest           80 numbers, 7 differ from generated canonical
    ScenarioDrakeTest       23 files, 0 rejected, 0 drifted, 23 clean
    SubtypeFactVisibility   CO-DECLARATION DROPS THE FACT             (categorical)

Two things the table says about itself, because a figure nobody is obliged to maintain is one nobody
can trust. First: a disagreement between the table and a run means either the run regressed or the
table went stale, and both are worth stopping for — so the commit that legitimately moves a number
updates the table in the same commit, exactly as the CHANGELOG entry and the record already ride
along. Second: the alpha.6 notes' 16 is left standing, because it was true when written; that drift
is the thing the table exists to make visible rather than something to paper over.

The scenario's gates are deliberately absent from it. They used to be report-only and now assert —
projects, wires, runs. Moving a measurement into an assertion is the goal, and the table should
shrink as that happens. It is the cheap half of #62, not a substitute for it.
